Context
The system must support multiple large tenants (e.g., banks, telecoms, airlines) while ensuring strict data isolation, scalability, and compliance with GDPR. The challenge is choosing the right multi-tenant database strategy.

Decision
Adopt a shared database with tenant-scoped tables, using a tenant_id column on all tenant-specific records. The application enforces isolation at the service and repository layers.

Rationale
Reduces operational overhead (one DB rather than many)
Supports high-volume tenants with optimised indexes
Allows simpler reporting and backups
Avoids code duplication required in separate-DB-per-tenant models

Consequences
Strong tenant filtering is mandatory on all queries
Security testing must validate no cross-tenant leakage
Future scaling to per-tenant databases remains possible
Alternatives Considered
Dedicated DB per tenant → higher cost, complex migrations
Schema-per-tenant → increases maintenance overhead
